WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.290
We might know the story of how young Jason
Voorhees rose from the deep to slaughter generations

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:06.670
of hormonal teens.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.570
But we may not know much about the actor who
played the hockey-loving homicidal maniac.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.889
There's a lot more to being a cinematic monster
than waving a prop machete at a babysitter.

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:18.320
It's time we turn off the screams for a little
bit and look at the horror movie characters

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.510
behind the mask.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:21.510
Pinhead

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.890
Although writer Clive Barker didn't name the
lead bad guy "Pinhead" in his novel The Hellbound

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.570
Heart, the character's distinctive appearance
earned him the nickname that stuck throughout

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.470
the Hellraiser series, which first launched
in 1987.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.789
English actor Doug Bradley has become synonymous
with the soul-harvesting Pinhead.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.560
"Someone else you think escaped us, like Frank?"

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.600
"No, this one didn't escape.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.640
It's you!"

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.100
He's become part of a special group of horror
actors who have played their haunting characters

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.239
at least six times in a row, a list that includes
Jigsaw played by Tobin Bell, the Leprechaun

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:03.899
played by Warwick Davis, Brad Dourif as Chucky,
and, well, this next guy...

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:05.309
Freddy Krueger

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:09.420
As much as we liked Jackie Earle Haley in
that Nightmare on Elm Street remake, only

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:15.350
one actor's name will always truly be associated
with our horror movie nightmares: Robert Englund.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.030
"Aw, bitch, I don't know what to say!"

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.590
We've seen Englund wear the glove for nearly
two decades, playing Freddy Krueger in eight

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:27.130
films and even in the character's own anthology
horror series, Freddy's Nightmares.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.789
Sure, Freddy got a lot funnier and sillier
as the years went by…

00:01:30.789 --> 00:01:32.499
"Bon appetit, bitch!"

00:01:32.499 --> 00:01:37.289
...but Wes Craven's New Nightmare was a proper
return to form for the supernatural slasher.

00:01:37.289 --> 00:01:42.499
Hopefully Englund puts on the fedora, sweater,
and glove one last time for a final trip down

00:01:42.499 --> 00:01:44.369
Elm Street.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:45.700
Leatherface

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.090
Tobe Hooper's kooky 1974 horror flick The
Texas Chainsaw Massacre spawned all kinds

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.189
of weird sequels, but there's no comparison
to the original.

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:59.450
Heavily inspired by the horrific true story
of Ed Gein, Leatherface wore a headpiece made

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:00.610
of human skin.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.979
While plenty of people have picked up the
chainsaw since, Gunnar Hansen was the original

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:06.479
actor to take on the role.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:10.450
Hansen set the standard for the other Leatherfaces
who've followed, laying the groundwork for

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.910
how to properly play the chainsaw-wielding
maniac.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.220
Fans of the B movie Mosquito will also recognize
Hansen as one of its main characters — who

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.150
actually uses a chainsaw to fight off enormous,
blood-sucking bugs.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:26.720
That's a lot of chainsaws… but has anyone
ever seen the guy cut down a tree?

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.290
The Alien

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.349
Ridley Scott's Alien revolutionized both the
science fiction and horror movie genres in

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:33.349
1979.

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:37.700
In the beginning, no one knew who was going
to be cast as the film's terrifying monster

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.480
until Scott was introduced to Bolaji Badejo,
a design student from Nigeria.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.900
Badejo's thin frame and seven-foot-two height
would help convince viewers that the film's

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.530
monster couldn't just be another guy in a
suit.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:53.629
While he only played the alien in the first
film of the franchise, he still left a heavy

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:54.629
impression.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:58.879
Badejo's alien is just a bodysuit, but he
ended up doing an amazing job to help sell

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:03.049
the illusion, and the creature would go on
to become one of Hollywood's most unforgettable

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:08.040
monsters, right up there with Frankenstein,
Dracula, and Randy Quaid.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.040
The Predator

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.910
Originally, Jean-Claude Van Damme was cast
to play the Predator in the 1987 flick of

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:14.990
the same name.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.410
Unfortunately, the bulked-out appearance of
star Arnold Schwarzenegger and the rest of

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:21.860
the cast made Van Damme seem way too small
for the role.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:25.580
How amazing it would have been if the Predator
had kung fu skills?

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:30.379
Instead, Kevin Peter Hall, who also starred
as the towering title character in Harry and

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:32.020
the Hendersons, snagged the role.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:36.730
When you've got guys like Jesse "The Body"
Ventura and Carl Weathers walking around a

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.470
jungle, you're going to want to make your
movie monster a behemoth, and not just the

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:41.819
muscles from Brussels.

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:45.930
Van Damme, you're great, but we'll call you
when we need a Predator that can do the splits.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:52.730
"Because of my big legs, and karate, I can
do the splits, no problem!"

00:03:54.599 --> 00:03:56.090
We know, dude.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.090
Pennywise

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:02.490
A fear of clowns is pretty common, and for
a generation of horror film fans, a lot of

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:07.800
that can be attributed to the 1990 made-for-TV
miniseries It, based on Stephen King's bestselling

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.239
horror novel.

00:04:09.239 --> 00:04:14.739
"When you're down here with me, you'll float
too!"

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:24.350
Pennywise the Dancing Clown, played by Tim
Curry, was just one of a few horrific forms

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:27.800
taken by the nefarious spirit that haunted
that small town in Maine.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.560
By this point in his career, Curry was known
for playing the Lord of Darkness in Legend

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.760
and Dr. Frank-N-Furter in The Rocky Horror
Picture Show.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.120
Curry's captivating, terrifying performance
was the miniseries' saving grace, despite

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.310
the gorier parts being hindered by TV restrictions.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:47.800
Whatever they do with the upcoming remake,
Bill Skarsgård has some rather large shoes

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:51.320
to fill—and not just because he's a clown.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:52.940
Jason Voorhees

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.970
Ten actors have played the character of Jason
Voorhees, but we're going to have to choose

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:03.980
Kane Hodder as the definitive hockey-masked
maniac.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.820
While he didn't even sign onto the series
until Friday the 13th Part VII: The New Blood,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:13.740
Hodder's role helped bring Jason into a new
age of slasher flicks and made him the hulking,

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.170
unstoppable menace that we all love today.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:20.730
He's also had the most experience playing
Jason, wearing the mask in four films.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.110
Hodder remains a horror movie heavyweight
champion.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:28.460
He also kinda played Leatherface once...as
a stuntman in The Texas Chainsaw Massacre

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:29.690
III.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.530
Michael Myers

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:37.090
John Carpenter's low-budget 1978 slasher classic
Halloween added a heavy psychological aspect

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:41.650
to the psycho that we hadn't seen since Norman
Bates decades prior, and turned the film's

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:47.180
fictional idyllic suburb into an unspeakably
creepy place after the sun set on October

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.180
thirty-first.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.650
Nick Castle, who is primarily a director and
screenwriter, may have only donned the mask

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:57.440
in the first movie, but his awesome performance
as Michael Myers would be imitated for years.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.990
Castle even co-wrote Carpenter's dystopian
action flick, Escape from New York, helped

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:06.440
write the story for Steven Spielberg's Hook,
and has directed a long list of movies that

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.090
includes The Last Starfighter.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:09.220
And now for a bonus fact!

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:14.020
Now, we just talked about who was first under
that Michael Myers mask, but what about the

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.150
face behind the mask itself?

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:18.560
None other than William Shatner.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.460
That's right: a mold of Captain Kirk's face
terrorized Jamie Lee Curtis.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:27.210
And then Shatner himself decided to terrorize
the houses in his neighborhood when he went

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.670
trick or treating with his kids:

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:35.780
"Then I bought one, and took the three of
you trick or treating, and put on my own mask.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:36.420
Trick or treat!"

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:37.740
Thanks for watching!

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.390
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:42.690
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

